================================================================================
 LUCID — JEPA-for-text : Per-Sentence Prediction Architecture (target design)
================================================================================

 The three subsystems: REASONER (predicts next) --- WRITER (turns concept
 into words) with the ENCODER between them at every boundary.

 Legend:
   [BOX]      module
   (dim)      tensor shape
   `----->    data/control flow
   FWD        "free-run" generation path marks the REASONING loop


┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ 1. ENCODER  — turn text into a 512-dim concept vector (the "meaning blob")  │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                                                                               
   sentence text:  "The kitchen door creaked open."
        │
        ▼
   ┌─────────────────────┐
   │  tokenizer (gpt2)   │  ids  ──►  [BOS] The kitchen door ... [EOS]
   └─────────┬───────────┘
             ▼
   ┌─────────────────────┐
   │ JEPA TextEncoder    │   6 layers, d_model 512, max 128 tokens
   │ (mean-pooled, L2    │
   │  normalized)        │
   └─────────┬───────────┘
             ▼
      concept vector  z_t   (512,)  =  the sentence's meaning blob
             │
             ▼
   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ ANTI-COLLAPSE REPAIR (so it stays meaningful)     │
   │  • variance/cov on window-disjoint sample+FIFO   │
   │  • uniformity with neighbor-mask (protect adj)   │
   │  • adjacent-sentence InfoNCE (pay for closeness) │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────┘


┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ 2. REASONER  — predict the NEXT sentence's concept (the REASONING step)     │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                                                                               
   history:   z_1  z_2  ...  z_t          (sequence of sentence vectors, T<=32)
              │    │         │
              └────┴─────────┴──────────►  ContextTransformer trunk
                                            (causal, 6 layers, d_model 512)
                                            │
                                            ▼
                                       hidden states h_t
                                            │
                         ┌──────────────────┴───────────────────┐
                         ▼                                      ▼
            ┌──────────────────────┐              ┌──────────────────────────┐
            │   vMF head (K=1)     │              │   MDN head (K=8 vMF)      │
            │   mu(c)  (512,)      │              │   pi_k mu_k kappa_k       │
            │   kappa (1,) strength│              │   (learned multimodality) │
            └──────────┬───────────┘              └────────────┬──────────────┘
                       │                                        │
                       │      SAMPLE (this is NOVELTY):         │
                       │      draw x̂ from vMF(mu, kappa/tau)    │
                       │      kappa = coherence dial            │
                       ▼                                        ▼
                    x̂_{t+1}  (512,)  = novel predicted next concept
                       │
                       │   (FWD: one prediction completes one REASONING step)
                       ▼
   ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ 3. WRITER  — two-channel, render the novel concept as real words    │
   └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

   CHANNEL 1 (local coherence):            CHANNEL 2 (steering/novelty):
   previous sentence's TOKENS              the predicted concept vector x̂
   ──────────────────────────              ────────────────────────────────
   [BOS] The kitchen door ... [EOS]  ┐
                                     ├─► combined input ids + concept embed
   (entity carry-over, style)        │        at position 0
                                     ┘
        ┌───────────────────────────────────────────┐
        │  FluWriter (152M, GPT-2-style)            │
        │  • NOTICE: we also DO NOT use the auto-   │
        │  •  per-block FiLM: concept re-injected   │
        │     into ALL 12 blocks (was 3)            │
        └──────────────────┬────────────────────────┘
                           ▼
                    new sentence text:
       "She froze at the sound from the back of the stairwell."
                           │
                           ▼
        ┌────────────────────────────────────────────┐
        │ CLOSED-LOOP: encode new sentence -> z_{t+1}│
        │ feed back into reasoner history.           │
        │ (free-run reasoning continues, step t+2...)│
        └────────────────────────────────────────────┘


================================================================================
 HOW IT GENERATES — the REASONING loop, step by step
================================================================================

   STEP 0  (seed)   encode prompt sentences → context vectors z_1..z_t
                              ↓
   STEP 1  (reason) reasoner trunk reads history → head emits  mu, kappa
                              ↓
   STEP 2  (sample) x̂ = sample vMF(mu, kappa/tau)   ← NOVEL POINT (new thought)
                              ↓
   STEP 3  (write)  writer takes  [prev tokens] + [x̂ via all-block FiLM]
                              →  generates next sentence
                              ↓
   STEP 4  (re-encode)  encode("new sentence") = z_{t+1} ;  append to history
                              ↓
   STEP 5  repeat STEP 1..4  →  a document unfolds, one novel step at a time

   KEY PROPERTY: the writer is GENERATIVE (samples tokens, not retrieval) and
   the concept channel is SAMPLED (not nearest-neighbor) — so every pass can
   produce a sentence that was never written. Local coherence comes from the
   previous sentence's WORDS; global arc/novelty comes from the SAMPLED concept.

   tau (temperature over kappa) is the coherence↔novelty dial:
     tau < 1  →  sharper   (stay on topic)
     tau = 1  →  learned   (as the model calibrated it)
     tau > 1  →  broader   (more novel, more risk of drift)


